## Module 2: Computer Fundamentals
*Rooms: Inside a Computer System · Computer Types · Client-Server Basics · Virtualisation Basics · Cloud Computing Fundamentals*

### 2.1 Inside a Computer System
Core hardware components and why each one matters for security work:

| Component | Function | Security relevance |
|-----------|----------|---------------------|
| **CPU** (Central Processing Unit) | Executes instructions; speed ≈ core count × clock speed | Bottleneck for brute-force/crypto operations |
| **RAM** (Random Access Memory) | Temporary, **volatile** working memory — wiped on reboot | Holds *active* processes, and often passwords/keys in plaintext while running → basis of RAM/memory forensics and cold-boot attacks |
| **Storage** (HDD/SSD) | **Persistent** — holds the OS, files, apps | Where forensic evidence and artefacts live long-term |
| **Motherboard** | Physically connects every component | — |
| **GPU** (Graphics Processing Unit) | Massively parallel processing | Used (by both sides) for fast password/hash cracking |
| **NIC** (Network Interface Card) | Connects the machine to a network | Provides the device's **MAC address** |
| **PSU** (Power Supply Unit) | Converts mains power to component-usable power | — |

### 2.2 Computer Types
- **Desktop/Laptop** — general-purpose, end-user machines; the most common attack surface (phishing, malware delivery).
- **Server** — usually headless (no GUI/monitor), always-on, runs services other devices connect to (web, database, file servers).
- **Mobile devices** — smartphones/tablets (iOS/Android); still "just a computer" but with a different OS and app model.
- **Embedded / IoT devices** — purpose-built hardware (routers, cameras, smart thermostats). Frequently under-secured — default credentials and unpatched firmware make them common targets.
- *Why it matters:* knowing the device type tells you what's likely exposed (a server's open services vs. a laptop's user-driven attack surface vs. an IoT device's default password).

### 2.3 Client-Server Basics
- **Client-server model:** the **client** requests a service; the **server** provides/responds to it.
- A single server can handle many clients at once.
- Examples: web browser (client) ↔ web server (HTTP) · email app (client) ↔ mail server (SMTP/IMAP) · SSH client ↔ SSH server.
- Contrast: **Peer-to-Peer (P2P)** — every node acts as both client *and* server (e.g., BitTorrent) — no central server.

### 2.4 Virtualisation Basics
- **Virtualisation** = running multiple independent "virtual" computers (**VMs**) on one physical machine.
- **Hypervisor** = the software that creates and manages VMs:
  - **Type 1 (bare-metal)** — runs directly on hardware (VMware ESXi, Hyper-V) — used in datacentres/enterprise.
  - **Type 2 (hosted)** — runs on top of a host OS (VirtualBox, VMware Workstation) — used for personal labs.
- Why it matters in security: an isolated, disposable environment to safely detonate malware, test exploits, or run vulnerable practice machines (exactly what TryHackMe itself does with target VMs!) without risking the host.
- **Snapshots** — save a VM's exact state so you can instantly revert after something goes wrong (or after intentionally breaking it).
- **Containers** (e.g. Docker) — a *lighter-weight* alternative that shares the host OS kernel instead of virtualising full hardware — faster to spin up, less isolated than a full VM.

### 2.5 Cloud Computing Fundamentals
- **Cloud computing** = renting computing resources over the internet instead of owning physical hardware.
- Service models (control vs. convenience trade-off):

| Model | You manage | Provider manages | Example |
|-------|-----------|-------------------|---------|
| **IaaS** (Infrastructure as a Service) | OS, runtime, apps, data | Physical hardware, virtualisation | AWS EC2, Azure VMs |
| **PaaS** (Platform as a Service) | Application code & data | OS, servers, runtime | Heroku, AWS Elastic Beanstalk |
| **SaaS** (Software as a Service) | Just your data/config | Everything else | Gmail, Microsoft 365 |

- Deployment models: **Public cloud** (shared infrastructure — AWS/Azure/GCP), **Private cloud** (dedicated to one organisation), **Hybrid cloud** (mix of both).
- **Shared Responsibility Model** — the provider secures the cloud *itself* (physical datacentres, hypervisor); the customer secures what they put *in* the cloud (data, access permissions, configuration). Misconfigured cloud storage (e.g. a public S3 bucket) is one of the most common real-world breach causes.
- Major providers: **AWS**, **Microsoft Azure**, **Google Cloud Platform (GCP)**.

